diagnoses.fixSketch:
The offset arithmetic is not merely wrong, it is unnecessary. `String.split` returns `[Substring]`, and a Substring carries indices into its *base* string. Each word already knows exactly where it is; the counter can be deleted rather than corrected.

In `extractTextWithSpatialOrdering` (DocumentProcessor.swift:7671):

1. Delete `var wordIndex = 0` (7685) and delete line 7721 entirely.
2. Replace the range construction at 7691 with the base-relative, UTF-16-correct conversion already used at LayoutAwareExtractor.swift:297:

```swift
for word in words {
    let wordString = String(word)
    guard let nsRange = NSRange(word.startIndex..<word.endIndex, in: pageString) else {
        unresolvedWords += 1
        continue
    }
    if let selection = page.selection(for: nsRange),
       let firstChar = selection.selectionsByLine().first {
        ...
    } else {
        unresolvedWords += 1
    }
}
```

`NSRange(_:in:)` must take `in: pageString` — the base — not `in: word`, or the offsets become word-relative and the bug returns in a new form. It is failable, hence the `guard let`. This kills both error sources at once: no separator counting, so whitespace runs are irrelevant; and the conversion emits UTF-16 code-unit offsets, so graphemes never enter the NSRange.

3. Make the discard audible, per the silent-truncation rule. Add `var unresolvedWords = 0`, increment it on both failure branches above, and before returning:

```swift
if unresolvedWords > 0 {
    Log.warning("[DocumentProcessor] Spatial extraction dropped \(unresolvedWords)/\(words.count) words (no selection bounds)", category: .ingestion)
}
```

4. Make the bail-out at 7736 audible too — today it silently hands the caller a raw across-the-columns read:

```swift
guard spatialLines.count > 3 else {
    Log.warning("[DocumentProcessor] Spatial extraction yielded only \(spatialLines.count) lines from \(words.count) words; falling back to unordered page.string", category: .ingestion)
    return nil
}
```

That warning is what turns this row from invisible into diagnosable on the next device trace, and it is the line that would have caught the defect originally.

Scope discipline: stop there. Two adjacent weaknesses are real but are *not* this root cause and should not be bundled into the same change, because doing so would make the fix unverifiable — (a) `detectColumnBoundaries` clusters on line `midX` (7717, 7739) rather than left edge `minX`, so ragged right margins blur the gutter and a full-width heading centred near the page middle gets assigned to the right-hand column by the `>` test at 7758; (b) `currentLineBounds.isEmpty` at 7718 uses `CGRect.isEmpty`, which is true for zero width *or* height, so it is an imperfect "not yet initialised" sentinel. Land the offset fix alone, confirm the reading order on a real two-column PDF, then judge (a) against the corrected baseline.

verdict.correction:
Keep the bug, drop the story, and fix the fix.

WHAT TO ACTUALLY CLAIM. `extractTextWithSpatialOrdering` (DocumentProcessor.swift:7671) computes NSRange offsets in the wrong unit and with a wrong separator width, so word geometry is misattributed by an amount that grows down the page. That is a genuine, live defect on the shipping path (4207). It is NOT established as the cause of "two-column PDFs reach the raw text path", and it should not be filed against that row without a device trace showing 7736 firing.

CORRECTED FIX (the arithmetic-deletion idea is right, the syntax is not). Delete `var wordIndex = 0` (7685) and line 7721. `NSRange(_:in:)` for StringProtocol is non-failable in this SDK, so it is a plain expression, not a `guard let`:

    for word in words {
        let wordString = String(word)
        let nsRange = NSRange(word.startIndex..<word.endIndex, in: pageString)
        if let selection = page.selection(for: nsRange),
           let firstChar = selection.selectionsByLine().first { ... } else { unresolvedWords += 1 }
    }

`in: pageString` (the base) is right and load-bearing — `in: word` would produce word-relative offsets and reintroduce the bug. Declare `var unresolvedWords = 0` alongside `spatialLines` at 7677, before first use. `Log.warning(_:category:)` is real (LoggingConfiguration.swift:368, `typealias Log` at :416) and `.ingestion` exists (:86), so both proposed warnings compile.

ADD ONE LOG THE DIAGNOSIS DID NOT ASK FOR, because it is the one that would actually settle the row. Before the `?? text` at 4207 there is no way to tell nil-return from success. Log the drift itself: emit the final `wordIndex` against `pageString.utf16.count` (or, post-fix, log `spatialLines.count` and `words.count` unconditionally at debug). Without that, "the offset bug caused the raw fallback" stays unfalsifiable.

FIX THE DEAD COMPARISON SEPARATELY AND SAY IT IS SEPARATE. DocumentProcessor.swift:3566 `pageText != pageData.pageString` is always false because 3500 assigns one from the other. Even though the enclosing function is dead for PDFs today, the same never-true pattern is what makes any future spatial-vs-native trace claim untrustworthy. File it as its own row: "spatial extraction is never reported as spatial; `usedSpatial` is a constant false."

RECONSIDER THE ROW'S REAL MECHANISM. A more direct explanation for "reads across the columns" is 7743-7746: when `detectColumnBoundaries` returns empty, the function sorts every line by Y alone and returns non-nil, which for a two-column page interleaves left and right lines — an across-the-columns read with no fallback and no log. `detectColumnBoundaries` (7784) also needs `xPositions.count > 5`, clusters on line midX rather than left edge, and `prefix(2)` at 7803 will split a two-column page carrying a full-width heading into three columns. The diagnosis explicitly defers all of this as "not this root cause"; on the evidence, it is at least as likely to BE the root cause. Correct the offsets first because they are unambiguously wrong, but do not close the row on that basis.

VERIFIABILITY, HONESTLY. Nothing here is provable without a device or simulator run on a real two-column PDF: the repo has no test touching `extractTextWithSpatialOrdering`, PDFKit selection geometry cannot be exercised in a unit test without a fixture PDF, and the existing ingestion trace cannot distinguish spatial from native on any live path. The only pre-fix proof available is a device trace with the drift log added. Treat "fixed" as unconfirmed until a two-column PDF's extracted text is read by eye.

HARD BOUNDARIES: none. The fix touches only DocumentProcessor.swift. project.pbxproj was read to confirm the deployment target, not modified.
